 but I want to be  more specific than that because usually  when people think Tim Burton visually  they think you know Beetlejuice they  think you know the the Sleepy Hollow you  know the sort of darker I mean like the  more bright and of Tim Burton I mean  like the stuff set in the suburban parts  for Edward Scissorhands I mean like big  fish I mean like even Willy Wonka Karly  in the Chocolate Factory was his version  so it has that kind of feel to the  visuals which I also think again adds  very much to the atmosphere of the whole  thing as not in any way trying to feel  like it's set in the real world which is  good because none of this is even  vaguely realistic in any way shape or  form we also have a few other cool  supporting characters that I quite  enjoyed there because Ned is actually a  pie maker that is theoretically his day  job he owns a pie shop called the pie  hole  but the reason he hooked up with Emerson  is he he wasn't really making his rent  with that so he does in fact need the  extra money but his his main employee  there played by Kristen Chenoweth has a  massive crush on him which is not  reciprocated I think this might have  actually been the first thing I saw  Kristen Chenoweth in now that I think of  it net also has a dog which was his  childhood dog that he brought back from  the dead and he now can't can't touch  again so like he has the this this sort  of puppet a this puppeted little hand on  a stick that he holds out lights like to  pet the dog because he can't touch the  dog and so there's there's things like  that and the end the guest casts are fun  and the in the murders that they solve  are kind of nuts and ridiculous  including Chuck's chocolate chucks is  kind of ridiculous and we  we get these interesting backstories to  the guest characters in the case of the  very first episode - you know Chuck's  aunts who are involved in sort of  solving everything and then in the  second episode - not only the person who  died but this 